 **Parameters:**
-*   **Dominant Axis Selection:** Based on the geometric normal vector. Not given in the sources.
-*   **Clamping:** Extents are clamped to pixel extents. Not given in the sources.
+*   **Resolution:** $(V_x, V_y, V_z)$.
+*   **Straddle Condition:** $d_{min} < d_{voxel} < d_{max}$ or $d_{max} < d_{voxel} < d_{min}$.
 
-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Degeneracy:** Triangles parallel to the projection direction may project as lines. The method relies on the dominant axis selection to avoid this for the primary projection, but edge tests handle remaining ambiguities [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Precision:** Depth evaluation must be precise to determine if values are on different sides of a voxel center [S1, unofficial].
+**Edge Cases:**
+*   **Degenerate Triangles:** If the triangle normal is zero, the dominant axis is undefined. [Not given in the sources.]
+*   **Precision:** Floating-point errors in depth evaluation may cause incorrect straddle decisions. [G] Use epsilon comparisons for straddle tests.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Proportional to the number of pixels covered by the projected triangle. One rasterization pass instead of three.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Same as standard rasterization-based voxelization, storing the voxel grid.
-*   **Strengths:** Reduces the number of 2D projections from three to one, potentially improving performance.
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the thinness property and correct identification of the dominant axis. May require careful handling of edge cases where the triangle is nearly parallel to the projection plane [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot R)$, where $N$ is the number of triangles and $R$ is the number of pixels covered by the projection. Significantly faster than multi-view methods as it performs only one rasterization pass per triangle.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V_x \cdot V_y \cdot V_z)$ for the voxel grid.
+*   **Strengths:** High performance due to single projection; produces 6-separating voxelization.
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the "thinness" property, which may not hold for all geometries or resolutions. [Not given in the sources.]
+*   **When to use:** When performance is critical and the geometry is well-behaved (thin).
+*   **When not to use:** For thick geometries where the dominant axis projection may miss voxels that would be captured by other axes.
 
 ## Background
-This method is derived from the thinness property and the equivalence of 3-axis rasterization with the crosshairs target [S1, unofficial]. It was presented in "A Topological Approach to Voxelization" by Laine et al. [S1, unofficial]. The approach simplifies the voxelization process by exploiting the fact that the dominant axis projection is sufficient for most pixels, with edge tests handling the rest.
+This method is derived from the topological approach to voxelization, specifically leveraging the equivalence of 3-axis rasterization with the crosshairs target [S1, unofficial]. It was presented in the context of producing tunnel-free voxelization efficiently. The concept builds on earlier work by Laine et al. [FBP09, CNS*11] regarding crosshair-based voxelization.
 
